\pard\sa100{\f0\fs22\b Persistence Model:\par}
\pard\sa200\li720{\f0\fs20 The API route is currently \b stateless\b0  in-flight. Frontend persistence uses localStorage (`phishTank_history`), with Convex database tables ready for centralized logging upon auth activation.\par}

\pard\sa200{\f2\fs28\b\cf2 4. Technical Gaps & Limitations for Commercial B2B\par}

\pard\sa100\li720{\f0\fs20 1. \b Short URLs & Redirection Chains:\b0  No upfront HTTP 3xx redirection hop tracing before launching headless browser.\par}
\pard\sa100\li720{\f0\fs20 2. \b Anti-Bot & Cloaking Evasion:\b0  Basic headless browser flags are detectable by modern phishing kits which serve safe pages to bots.\par}
\pard\sa100\li720{\f0\fs20 3. \b Threat Intel Feeds:\b0  Lacks third-party threat feed enrichment (Google Safe Browsing, VirusTotal, URLhaus).\par}
\pard\sa100\li720{\f0\fs20 4. \b Serverless Resource Heavy:\b0  Launching Chromium per request causes high latency (5-15s) and memory consumption under concurrency.\par}
\pard\sa100\li720{\f0\fs20 5. \b Rate Limiting & Auth:\b0  No tenant-level token bucket rate limiting on /api/scan, exposing backend to quota exhaustion.\par}
\pard\sa200\li720{\f0\fs20 6. \b Client/Server Mismatch:\b0  Guest UI uses simulated scans rather than unified routing to the backend scan engine.\par}

